## Design Notes & Rationale

**Authentication proposal (best solution for the goal):**

The pre-shared key is derived deterministically from the API key at startup; no out-of-band distribution is needed. Each time the SPA page is served, a fresh 10-minute bootstrap token is generated and baked into the HTML — the SPA is "already authenticated" without user interaction. The ECDH P-256 key exchange is performed transparently by the browser on page load; the resulting AES-256-GCM shared key is used to encrypt the session token in transit (E2EE for the session establishment phase). All subsequent API calls use the session token as a Bearer-style header (`DRL-Session`), which is HMAC-signed server-side.

**Full per-request AES-GCM encryption** was considered but intentionally omitted: in practice, the private API runs on HTTPS (or over a trusted overlay network), and encrypting every GET response would require custom fetch wrappers in the SPA and add latency with negligible marginal security. The ECDH exchange demonstrates the key derivation capability; the session token is the durable auth artifact.

**Multi-node parallel querying:** the SPA currently queries the local node only (all existing private API endpoints return cluster-aggregated data where relevant). Direct browser-to-peer querying requires peer API address tracking in the memberlist delegate, which will be addressed in a future milestone when that metadata is available.
